From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 16:40:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] skw: sleep and wake on the Bluetooth remote

A board whose only input is a BLE remote has to suspend on its power key and
wake on any key. This one could do neither: every suspend ended within seconds,
and nothing the remote did could bring the box back.

Deep suspend cannot filter a wake. The SoC resumes in hardware before any
instruction runs, so there is no point at which a packet can be examined and
judged. It does not need to be. The chip pulls host-wake only when it has
something to hand over, so the question is not which wakes to allow but which
packets the controller should ever produce - and that is configurable from the
host with commands the core spec already defines.

Six faults, one path:

  hdev->wakeup was never set, so hci_suspend_sync() took its early return and
  armed nothing. Bluetooth could not wake the host at all.

  send_host_suspend_indication() sampled the chip's "traffic pending" GPIO once,
  and suspend generates traffic of its own, so the single sample was taken at
  the moment the line was most likely high. The chip was never told the host had
  gone down and kept asserting it. Measured low 88% of the time while idle, yet
  high at every suspend; now it waits up to 200 ms for the line to settle.

  hci_suspend_sync() disconnects every link, and this handset answers with
  directed advertising within about three seconds. Waking on a bonded device's
  advertisement is upstream's design and cannot be told apart from a keypress,
  because it is the same PDU. keep_link_suspended borrows
  HCI_QUIRK_NO_SUSPEND_NOTIFIER to leave the link alone instead - off-label, as
  the quirk exists for controllers that leave the bus during suspend.

  Authenticated Payload Timeout Expired fired 655 s after the last keypress and
  capped every sleep there. The bit lives in event mask page 2, which HCI_INIT
  writes once and no suspend path revisits, so the page is cleared - nothing
  here consumes the event.

  The remote stops answering after roughly an hour and the controller times the
  link out; Disconnect Complete then pulls host-wake. Masking it is the whole
  fix, and masking it alone left this board unwakeable for 19.6 h, because the
  host re-enables accept-list scanning only in response to that same event. So
  presuspend_scan arms the scan first, while the link is still up, and
  mask_disconnect hides the event. The scan must use the extended commands where
  the core does - the two sets cannot be mixed, and the legacy ones are then
  refused with Command Disallowed - and the accept-list filter policy, or every
  advertiser in range becomes a wake source.

  The power key sends press and release about 360 ms apart, so suspending on the
  press left the release to arrive on the held link and end the sleep after
  0.37 s. A link that has only just been re-established is busy in the same way
  and does not survive being suspended into. settle_ms waits for quiet on any
  received packet - data alone sails through the gaps between setup events.

Everything the notifier sends runs under hdev->req_lock taken with a trylock
loop rather than mutex_lock. hci_cmd_sync() bounds the command but not the lock,
and a notifier that blocks there never returns: a second after a resume the core
still holds it for its own reconnect work. A suspend that finds the stack busy
goes down unarmed, which costs an early wake; one that blocks costs a power cut.

On resume the mask is restored and the stack reconciled: the host still believes
in a link the controller has forgotten, so the handle is probed with Read RSSI
and a Disconnect Complete synthesised when it is gone. Unknown Connection
Identifier arrives as ERR_PTR(-ENOTCONN), so the command failing is the answer.

Measured on an H96 Max 3518D: 5934 s asleep against the 3598 s that had ended
every previous attempt, and nineteen consecutive suspend/wake cycles on the
power key where every wake carried HID data rather than a reconnect advert.

Not for merge as it stands. Masking a core event and driving the controller's
scan state behind the stack's back are both liberties, and every part is behind
a parameter that defaults off.

Signed-off-by: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
